@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Services
@implements IDisposable

@*
    AppLayout — shared shell for the authenticated app area.
    Renders the sidebar navigation + the page content side by side.
    Auth pages (Login, Signup, etc.) continue to use MainLayout.
*@

@* ── The app's one icon paint server ───────────────────────────────────────
     An SVG stroke cannot take a CSS gradient — `stroke` resolves a paint
     server, not a background — so the signature sweep has to exist as a
     document node and be referenced by id. Declared HERE, once per document
     and outside every shell branch, so a card head on any page (any role,
     any shell) can paint its glyph with `stroke="url(#motivaIconGradient)"`
     and every one of them is provably the SAME mark rather than N competing
     ids. The container class is `.motiva-ico-gradient` in motiva-base.css.

     `to left` in SVG terms is x1=1 → x2=0: violet at the reading start,
     cyan at the reading end, matching --motiva-gradient-signature. The stops
     are the token's own three brand hues, restated here only because a
     <stop> cannot read a CSS custom property in every engine.

     Rendered unconditionally rather than inside a branch: an icon that
     paints before its paint server exists renders with no stroke at all.

     THE ZERO SIZE IS ON THE ELEMENT, NOT ONLY IN A STYLESHEET, and that is
     load-bearing. An <svg> with no width/height has an intrinsic size of
     300x150 and it is an in-flow inline element, so until a rule takes it out
     of flow it pushes the whole shell — top bar, page header and all — 150px
     down the viewport. Leaving that to `.motiva-grad-def` in motiva-base.css
     alone made the layout depend on a global stylesheet being current: a
     browser holding a cached copy from before that rule existed rendered every
     canvas page with 150px of dead space above the top bar. The presentation
     attributes and the inline style below cannot be missed or cached away, so
     the node is guaranteed to occupy nothing. The class stays — it says why,
     and it is what a reader will search for. *@
<svg class="motiva-grad-def" width="0" height="0"
     style="position:absolute;width:0;height:0"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <defs>
        <linearGradient id="motivaIconGradient" x1="1" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="#6D28F5" />
            <stop offset="60%"  stop-color="#2E63E8" />
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#0FA8C2" />
        </linearGradient>
    </defs>
</svg>

@if (UsesCanvasShell)
{
    @*
        ── The finalized canvas shell — Student, Mentor AND Lecturer ─────
        All three finished experiences draw the same chrome: a washed
        canvas with a top bar (logo · centred pill nav · round action
        cluster) and no rail. Every `… final.dc.html` student screen and
        every `Motiva Mentor *.dc.html` screen puts exactly this at the
        top, so one branch serves all three and MotivaTopNav renders each
        role's own destinations from NavDefinitions.

        LECTURER WAS RESTORED HERE, NOT ADDED. The branch existed at
        22a7cd4 (with a duplicate LecturerTopNav in it) and was dropped
        when Student and Mentor were merged onto one branch. Everything
        downstream of it stayed: OverviewDashboardPage.razor.css and
        LecturerProjectsPage.razor.css both document "the shell already
        applies --motiva-canvas-padding-inline", and motiva-tokens.css's
        `.motiva-lecturer` block zeroes --g-sidebar-width for "the same
        app-shell-student frame". So the rail a Lecturer was actually
        being given rendered at width: 0 — no navigation at all — and the
        pages rendered with no gutter. That is the concrete bug this
        branch fixes; nothing about GetShell, role resolution, the mode
        toggle or permissions changed.

        The reference draws its screens inside a centred, rounded canvas
        card floating on a darker page. That frame is deliberately NOT
        reproduced: the workspace uses the full viewport, and the canvas's
        soft wash becomes the page's own background instead. This is the
        one intentional deviation from the design files.

        What that means concretely — no max-width, no outer radius, no
        outer shadow, and no second background ringing the app. The
        chrome and the content sit directly on one washed surface.

        Kept as a separate branch rather than a variant of the sidebar
        shell below, because the two have no markup in common — one is a
        two-column flex with a rail, the other a single column. Branching
        here means Staff / Admin on the legacy shell render the exact
        tree they always have.

        The class stays `app-shell-student`: it is the selector ~60 lines
        of AppLayout.razor.css hang off, and renaming it would be a
        no-op diff across a stylesheet this epic has no other reason to
        touch. Read it as "the canvas shell".
    *@
    <div class="app-shell app-shell-student @ShellClasses">
        <MotivaTopNav />
        <div class="app-content">
            @Body
        </div>
    </div>
}
else
{
    @*  THERE IS NO SEPARATE LECTURER BRANCH BETWEEN THESE TWO, and there
        must not be one.

        A second branch stood here rendering `<LecturerTopNav />`. It was
        UNREACHABLE: `UsesCanvasShell` (above) already answers true for
        Shell.Lecturer, so `if (UsesCanvasShell)` claimed every lecturer
        before an `else if (IsLecturer)` could ever be tested.
        LecturerTopNav.razor itself was deleted when Lecturer was folded into
        MotivaTopNav — see that component's own header, "LECTURER IS A THIRD
        BRANCH HERE, NOT A THIRD COMPONENT" — so the branch also named a
        component that no longer exists.

        Razor cannot resolve the name and falls back to emitting it as a
        literal HTML element, which is why the build carried
        `RZ10012: Found markup element with unexpected name 'LecturerTopNav'`.
        Because the branch was dead the warning had no runtime effect:
        lecturers have always been served by the canvas branch above, and
        MotivaTopNav renders their eight destinations from
        NavDefinitions.GetNavItems like every other role. Removing it deletes
        dead code and the warning with it; no role, route or shell resolution
        changes.

        The comment lives INSIDE this block rather than between `}` and
        `else`: a Razor comment at that boundary compiles, but it sits on the
        transition between a code block and its continuation, and there is no
        reason to depend on how the parser treats it.  *@
    <div class="app-shell @ShellClasses">
        <AppSideNav />
        <div class="app-main">
            <AppTopBar />
            <div class="app-content">
                @Body
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private User? _user { get; set; }

    // Single attachment point for the Motiva System Master foundations.
    //
    // `.motiva` carries the entire token rebase declared in motiva-tokens.css
    // (palette, Heebo, type scale, radii, shadows, ambient gradients, shell
    // geometry). Putting it on the shell root means the sidebar AND the page
    // content inherit it together, and nothing outside this element can ever
    // reach it — so Lecturer, Staff and Admin continue to resolve every token
    // at its :root value, unchanged.
    //
    // TWO CLASSES, NOT ONE (Epic 0)
    // ─────────────────────────────
    // `.motiva`          the shared visual system. Every token, every
    //                    component override, every ambient gradient reads
    //                    this and only this. There is nothing in it that
    //                    means one thing for a student and another for a
    //                    mentor, which is exactly why it carries no role.
    //
    // `.motiva-student`  / `.motiva-mentor` — role hooks, for the places
    //                    where behaviour or UX genuinely differs. Nothing in
    //                    the foundations layer uses them today; they exist so
    //                    that when a genuine divergence appears it has an
    //                    obvious, scoped home instead of being expressed as a
    //                    second copy of the design system.
    //
    // The student experience is the regression baseline: it still carries
    // `.motiva-student`, and `.motiva` has identical specificity to the class
    // it replaced, declared in the same place in the same files — so every
    // computed value on every student page is what it was before.
    //
    // _user cascades in asynchronously from App.razor, so this resolves to
    // Staff on the very first render and settles once auth state arrives —
    // identical to the existing AppSideNav behaviour.
    //
    // Gated on NavDefinitions.GetShell, the same method AppSideNav uses for
    // its .snav-motiva rail and AppTopBar for its bell placement, so the
    // three can never disagree about who gets which shell — including the
    // dual-role (Staff + Mentor) case, where the active view mode decides.
    /// <summary>Which of the two shells renders. Reads the same
    /// NavDefinitions.GetShell as ShellClasses below, so the frame and the
    /// token scope can never disagree — including for a dual-role user, whose
    /// active mode decides.
    ///
    /// <para>Student, Mentor and Lecturer all resolve to the canvas shell:
    /// their finished designs draw identical chrome. Only Shell.Staff — the
    /// legacy fallback nothing routes to today — keeps the rail.</para></summary>
    private bool UsesCanvasShell => NavDefinitions.GetShell(_user)
        is NavDefinitions.Shell.Student
        or NavDefinitions.Shell.Mentor
        or NavDefinitions.Shell.Lecturer;

    /// <summary>
    /// THREE classes on the finished shells, not two.
    ///
    /// <para><c>motiva</c> — the shared visual system, as before.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><c>motiva-final</c> — the FINISHED design's values: the palette,
    /// geometry and component treatments transcribed from the Claude Design
    /// project. It carries no role because nothing in it is role-specific —
    /// the student, mentor and lecturer screens draw the same canvas, the same
    /// pills, the same 38px controls. All three finished experiences wear it.
    /// (It was named <c>motiva-student</c> until Mentor's designs landed with
    /// the same values — see the block it scopes in motiva-tokens.css.)</para>
    ///
    /// <para>LECTURER JOINED IT rather than keeping a private copy. The
    /// `.motiva-lecturer` block in motiva-tokens.css existed only to restate
    /// four canvas/nav tokens that are declared in `.motiva-final` — and it
    /// could not restate the ones it did not know about, so the Lecturer
    /// chrome resolved `.motiva`'s 24px radii and 40px controls instead of the
    /// design's 26px and 38px. Same class, same values, one declaration.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><c>motiva-student</c> / <c>motiva-mentor</c> / <c>motiva-lecturer</c>
    /// — the role hooks, unchanged in meaning: the place a genuine role
    /// divergence goes.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private string ShellClasses => NavDefinitions.GetShell(_user) switch
    {
        NavDefinitions.Shell.Student  => "motiva motiva-final motiva-student",
        NavDefinitions.Shell.Mentor   => "motiva motiva-final motiva-mentor",
        NavDefinitions.Shell.Lecturer => "motiva motiva-final motiva-lecturer",
        _                             => "",
    };

    // A dual-role user flipping the mode toggle changes which shell they are
    // in, not just which nav items they see. Without this the token scope
    // would keep whatever it resolved to at first render until a full reload.
    // AppSideNav already subscribes for the same reason.
    protected override void OnInitialized()
        => UserModeService.CurrentModeChanged += OnModeChanged;

    private void OnModeChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
        => UserModeService.CurrentModeChanged -= OnModeChanged;
}
